1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,763
A bundle of palm leaves, bound between two wooden boards.

2
00:00:04,763 --> 00:00:09,692
Here is the thing to understand before anything else: no original exists.

3
00:00:09,692 --> 00:00:11,058
Palm leaf rots.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,158
Every manuscript we have is a copy of a copy.

5
00:00:17,158 --> 00:00:25,411
What survived was not the object — it was the recitation, repeated exactly, generation after generation, and written down late.

6
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:32,704
One source only this time, and it is the texts themselves.

7
00:00:32,704 --> 00:00:37,845
This episode is a map of them, because once you can see how Vedic literature is arranged,

8
00:00:37,845 --> 00:00:42,029
a great deal of what looks like a jumble of names falls into place.

9
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:48,088
There are four Vedas.

10
00:00:48,088 --> 00:00:50,025
The Rigveda, hymns of praise.

11
00:00:50,025 --> 00:00:55,063
The Samaveda, those hymns set to melody — the root of Indian classical music.

12
00:00:55,063 --> 00:00:59,949
The Yajurveda, the formulas and procedures for performing a sacrifice.

13
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,648
And the Atharvaveda, which is different in character: charms, spells,

14
00:01:06,648 --> 00:01:10,852
remedies, and a great deal of everyday life that the others leave out.

15
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,941
Now the part that unlocks everything.

16
00:01:16,941 --> 00:01:20,989
Each Veda is not one text but four layers, built up over time.

17
00:01:20,989 --> 00:01:23,880
The Samhita is the core collection of hymns.

18
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:28,870
The Brahmanas are prose explanations of the rituals: what to do and why.

19
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:36,934
The Aranyakas, the forest books, are for those who have withdrawn from ordinary life,

20
00:01:36,934 --> 00:01:40,898
and the emphasis shifts from performing the ritual to understanding it.

21
00:01:40,898 --> 00:01:45,106
And the Upanishads sit innermost, where the question changes entirely.

22
00:01:48,767 --> 00:01:51,870
The Upanishads are philosophy, and mostly dialogue.

23
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:58,257
They ask what lies behind the ritual: what the self is, what reality is, and whether the two are the same.

24
00:01:58,257 --> 00:02:02,496
The word itself carries the sense of sitting down near a teacher.

25
00:02:05,667 --> 00:02:08,760
There are many; a smaller group are treated as principal.

26
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:14,926
This is the strand of Vedic thought that later Indian philosophy argues with for the next two thousand years.

27
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:26,135
And around the Vedas grew six supporting disciplines, the Vedangas, the limbs of the Veda.

28
00:02:26,135 --> 00:02:27,964
Shiksha, pronunciation.

29
00:02:27,964 --> 00:02:29,870
Kalpa, ritual procedure.

30
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:31,394
Vyakarana, grammar.

31
00:02:31,394 --> 00:02:34,823
Nirukta, the explanation of difficult words.

32
00:02:34,823 --> 00:02:36,343
Chhanda, metre.

33
00:02:39,967 --> 00:02:43,353
Jyotisha, astronomy, for fixing the time of a rite.

34
00:02:43,353 --> 00:02:51,884
Notice what these are: a civilisation that has to transmit a text perfectly by voice will invent phonetics, grammar and etymology.

35
00:02:51,884 --> 00:02:53,967
The precision was not academic.

36
00:02:53,967 --> 00:02:55,440
It was necessary.

37
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,321
Two words you will meet constantly.

38
00:03:00,321 --> 00:03:06,627
Shruti means that which was heard, and covers the Vedas and their layers — treated as revealed, and fixed.

39
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:19,451
Smriti means that which was remembered: the epics, the Puranas, the law books — human in authorship, and able to change.

40
00:03:19,451 --> 00:03:25,073
When a source is described as shruti or smriti, that is what is being claimed about it.

41
00:03:29,867 --> 00:03:33,310
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

42
00:03:33,310 --> 00:03:34,318
Four Vedas.

43
00:03:34,318 --> 00:03:35,410
Four layers.

44
00:03:35,410 --> 00:03:36,634
Six limbs.

45
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:42,307
Dig Quiz.

46
00:03:42,307 --> 00:03:43,524
Pause and name it.

47
00:03:43,524 --> 00:03:50,421
First: the Veda in which the hymns are set to melody, and from which Indian classical music is traced.

48
00:03:55,333 --> 00:03:56,289
The Samaveda.

49
00:03:56,289 --> 00:04:01,271
Second: the prose layer that explains how a ritual is performed and why.

50
00:04:01,271 --> 00:04:02,295
The Brahmanas.

51
00:04:02,295 --> 00:04:07,509
Third: the six supporting disciplines, from pronunciation to astronomy.

52
00:04:10,367 --> 00:04:11,253
The Vedangas.

53
00:04:11,253 --> 00:04:17,059
In Mains, a recurring question asks what the Upanishads contributed to Indian thought.

54
00:04:24,333 --> 00:04:31,259
Point to the shift from ritual performance to enquiry, and to the questions about the self and reality that later schools take up.

55
00:04:35,433 --> 00:04:41,101
A bundle of palm leaves takes its place in the gallery: the most carefully copied thing in this room.

56
00:04:44,733 --> 00:04:50,880
Next gallery: how this society ordered itself, and the arguments about how that order began.

57
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:54,947
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

